    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "California, with its extensive coastline and deep history of investment in renewable energy, may offer a feasible environment for commercial development of wave energy. This thesis evaluates the feasibility of California wave energy across technological, financial, and environmental fronts. Resource assessment reveals multiple locations that are both near the coast (< 30 km) and offer suitable wave conditions for power generation via Wave Energy Converters (WECs). The presence of Marine Protected Areas (MPAs) is accounted for, and does add a notable constraint to site selection. A site 6 km off the coast of northern California, near the city of Eureka, is chosen for analysis due to its proximity to shore and high annual omnidirectional wave power. Through financial analysis, it is determined that, even at this ideal site, WECs are currently prohibitively expensive given current cost estimates, electricity prices, and existing subsidies. Projecting into the future, an avenue towards financial feasibility is found if capital cost is assumed to decrease at a defined learning rate. Projections estimate that, without further subsidy, WECs will be financially feasible to deploy at the 10 MW scale by 2050. Analysis of a particular feed-in tariff subsidy suggests financial feasibility at smaller capacity levels with existing subsidy rates, subject to variations in wave conditions and uncertainties of cost. Various potential sustainability concerns, such as noise, leakage, and carbon emissions are considered, with none posing significant and convincing threats. Concluding insights identify opportunities for technological advancement, stress the financial advantage of deploying at scale and emphasize the need to establish more prosperous WEC revenue streams.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
